@block introduction | start | - | Start with the whole equality
@prose An equation says that its left expression and right expression have the same value. In this reading, x is a real unknown: a number not yet known. We will solve only equations of the form ax+b=c with a nonzero integer coefficient. The two balanced moves are removing the added constant and then removing the nonzero coefficient. Each move must affect complete sides of the equality.
@prose The six exercises move from reading notation to checking a calculation, choosing a goal-directed move, explaining an inverse, repairing a sign error and checking a fresh equation. Their compact choices name operations; this reading supplies the meaning and the reasons.
@endblock

@block definition | terms | 1.1 | Read ax+b=c before changing it
@prose In ax+b=c, the symbol x is the unknown and ranges over real numbers. The coefficient a is the number multiplying x. The added constant b is the separate number added to ax; it is not part of the coefficient. The right-hand constant c is the number after the equality sign. For example, in -4x+7=19, a is -4, b is 7 and c is 19.
@display eq_form
ax+b=c
@prose A solution is a real value of x that makes the original equality true after substitution. For a candidate r, substitute r for x and calculate ar+b. If that value equals c, r is a solution; otherwise it is not. Equality is the relation that the two complete expressions have the same value, not a permission to change only one expression.
@endblock

@block proposition | rule | 1.2 | A balanced operation preserves equality
@prose If u=v, subtracting the same number d from both complete sides gives u-d=v-d. Adding d to both complete sides reverses that operation. This inverse-operation pair explains why balance is preserved in both directions: no solution is lost and no new solution is introduced.
@display balance_rule
u=v\quad\Longrightarrow\quad u-d=v-d
@prose To remove b from ax+b=c, choose d=b. The left side becomes ax+b-b=ax, and the right side becomes c-b. Keep the signed b visible while calculating; do not silently change a subtraction into addition.
@display remove_constant
ax+b=c\quad\Longrightarrow\quad ax=c-b
@help proof
@prose Any x satisfying ax+b=c also satisfies ax+b-b=c-b after the same subtraction. Conversely, adding b to both sides of ax=c-b recovers ax+b=c. Therefore these two equations have exactly the same real solutions.
@endblock

@block example | signed_constant | 1.3 | Subtracting a negative adds its opposite
@prose A signed added constant must be named separately. In 3x-5=7, b=-5. Removing b means subtracting -5 from both sides. Subtracting a negative is adding its opposite, so the right side is 7-(-5)=7+5=12. The left side is 3x-5-(-5)=3x.
@display signed_move
3x-5=7\quad\Longrightarrow\quad3x=7-(-5)=12
@prose In contrast, writing 7-5=2 treats the added constant as +5 and changes the problem. Applying a move to only the left side is also invalid: it breaks the equality relation instead of producing an equivalent equation.
@endblock

@block proposition | condition | 1.4 | A nonzero coefficient has an inverse
@prose Once ax=d, divide both complete sides by a to isolate x. Division is valid here only when a is nonzero. Multiplying both sides by the same nonzero a reverses the division, so the solution set is preserved in both directions.
@display divide_rule
ax=d,\quad a\ne0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=\frac{d}{a}
@prose The nonzero condition matters. Division by zero is undefined, and multiplying an equation by zero can erase a restriction. For example, 3x=9 has the single solution x=3, but multiplying both sides by zero gives 0=0, a true statement for every real x. The original restriction cannot be recovered from 0=0 alone.
@display zero_boundary
0x=0\quad\text{is true for every real }x,\qquad0x=1\quad\text{is true for no real }x
@prose Those two boundary equations are explanatory cases, not playable cases in this unique-solution family. They show why this lesson requires a nonzero coefficient.
@endblock

@block example | worked | 1.5 | Worked example: solve 4x+3=11
@prose This separate example demonstrates both balanced moves. Its answer is not an exercise response. Here a=4, b=3 and c=11. First remove the added constant 3 from both complete sides.
@display worked_given
4x+3=11
@help hint
@prose Undo the addition of 3 by subtracting 3 from both complete sides. Then undo multiplication by the nonzero coefficient 4 by dividing both complete sides by 4.
@help answer
@display
x=2
@help solution
@prose Subtract 3 from both complete sides and evaluate the arithmetic on each side.
@display
4x+3-3=11-3
@display
4x=8
@prose Now divide both complete sides by 4. Because 4 is nonzero, multiplication by 4 is the inverse operation that can recover the preceding equation.
@display
\frac{4x}{4}=\frac{8}{4}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=2
@prose Check the candidate in the original given, including its original added constant: 4(2)+3=8+3=11. The equality holds. For completeness, if x1 and x2 both solved 4x+3=11, subtracting their equalities would give 4(x1-x2)=0; since 4 is nonzero, x1=x2. Thus x=2 is the unique real solution.
@endblock

@block example | errors | 1.6 | Diagnose the first error before repairing it
@prose A later line can follow an earlier mistake correctly. Read an attempt from top to bottom and identify the first line that ceases to represent an equivalent equation. In the deliberate attempt below, L2 correctly evaluates the expression written in L1 and L3 correctly divides that already-wrong equation. L1 is the first error because it replaces 7-(-5) with 7-5.
@display error_attempt
\begin{gathered}3x-5=7\\\begin{aligned}L_1 &: 3x=7-5\\L_2 &: 3x=2\\L_3 &: x=\frac{2}{3}\end{aligned}\end{gathered}
@help hint
@prose Read the signed added constant in the original equation. The operation that removes -5 is subtraction of -5, which adds 5 to the right side.
@prose Repairing the first line gives 3x=12, then x=4. A substitution into the original, 3(4)-5=7, checks the repair. The value 2/3 instead gives 3(2/3)-5=-3, not 7.
@endblock

@block exercise | practice | 1.7 | Prepare for the six choices
@prose In the exercises, gold givens show the original equation or a deliberately incorrect attempt. Cyan working records only a reached, accepted decision. A wrong choice leaves the working available and explains its particular issue. A correct choice advances one decision; green completion remains until Next. These display behaviours belong to the existing question owner.
@prose Read the stated goal as well as the equation. Adding 4 to both sides of -3x+4=13 preserves equality, but it does not remove the added constant; subtracting 4 from both complete sides does. The independent final question supplies no prescribed intermediate step. Choose only the value that makes its original equality true.
@endblock

@block summary | summary | 1.8 | Carry the reason with the arithmetic
@prose Name the unknown, coefficient, signed added constant and right-hand constant before operating. Remove the added constant by applying its inverse to both complete sides. Divide by the coefficient only when it is nonzero. Keep negative signs explicit, diagnose the earliest error, and substitute the result back into the original given.
@prose Definitions and equality conditions were checked against OpenStax, Elementary Algebra 2e, sections 2.1, Solve Equations Using the Subtraction and Addition Properties of Equality, and 2.2, Solve Equations using the Division and Multiplication Properties of Equality, accessed 2026-09-10. This reading's prose, examples, exercise cases and distractor explanations are original Paths material.
@endblock
